# Our own RSA login key, several during a rotation (server/README.md "Login encryption (RSA, XTEA)", docs/SECURITY_CHECKLIST.md).
# The 10.98 login sends the account, the password (the game login's session key holds it too) and the XTEA key of the
# session in one RSA block. The stock engine decrypts it with key.pem from the base download: the public default
# open-server key, whose private key everybody has. With it anyone on the path reads every login and can pose as the
# server. This patch lets the engine load our own key from outside the server folder:
#   * config.lua: rsaKeyFile = "key.pem" (the default, as before) or a path such as /etc/chaos/rsa/login.pem; several
#     files separated by commas are all accepted, the first is the current key (a rotation: new key first, the old one
#     after it until every player has the new client). Read at startup only; prod_config.py writes
#     rsaKeyFile = os.getenv("CHAOS_RSA_KEY_FILE") or "key.pem", so the path lives in /etc/chaos/chaos.env.
#   * Protocol::RSA_decrypt tries each key and takes the first whose result starts with the 0 byte the protocol
#     requires; a block no key opens is refused (disconnect), as before. A key that is not 1024 bits is refused at start.
#   * startup: one line per key with its fingerprint (the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 of the decimal modulus, the
#     same server/tools/rsa_key.py and client/tools/set_rsa_key.py print) and a WARNING when a key is the public default.
# Make the key with server/tools/rsa_key.py gen (on the game server, never in the repository). The client takes the
# public modulus at build time (client/tools/set_rsa_key.py, windows-package.yml secret CHAOS_RSA_PUBLIC).
# Apply in the server source root after 0001-0045: patch -p1 < 0046-own-rsa-login-key.patch, then rebuild.
